module gunbc.plans.deploy_convergence_observed_side

import std.dissolution { unbound_dissolution }
import std.types { List }
import gunbc.plan { Plan, PlanRetiresWhen, PlanIsAuthorityOnly }
import std.markdown { MarkdownBlock }
import gunbc.plans.md_helpers { p }
import gunbc.design_ruling { project_documentation_projections_are_not_checkout_artifacts }

fn deploy_convergence_observed_side_body() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    p(text: "**Goal/current direction:** deploy a coherent release, stage and observe it before the smallest cutover, monitor afterward, and execute rollback before claiming it. The 2026-10-17 operator direction follows the 2026-10-14 half-installed release outage. Extend gunbc.live_deploy.release_locus to dashboard and belt: one immutable content-addressed generation contains executable, source tree, rendered surfaces and coupled configuration. Host policy/durable state (slices, users, ports, journals, attempt records) remain outside and are never generation-owned. This design is not an actuation receipt."),
    p(text: "**Method:** build once; stage that generation on the srv1 lab tracking main, not lab-dev; require surface identity, dispatch digest and release-binding readback; promote the SAME generation without rebuilding. ExecStart and working directory use concrete generation paths, never a moving current symlink. The active-generation record names exactly one generation after failure; retain N and N-1 so convergence resumes and rollback activates N-1 through the same mechanism. Model in gunbc.live_deploy before migrating stages, on its own replacement integration branch: build→lab→cutover plus belt-admission replacement→executed lab rollback."),
    p(text: "**Admission/quiescence:** EffectDerivation closes all effects before first mutation; immutable generation facts bind build-time premises. Under generations, publication does not modify a tree any running member reads. Quiescence and exclusive deployment lease therefore narrow to unit rewrite/restart cutover, with volatile manager/port checks under the lease; failure leaves the prior generation active. The operation must require a quiescence guard, the parent transaction acquires/publishes/verifies/restores automatically, and steady-state belt admission consumes coherent generation identity. Persistent inhibition survives a failed cutover; all release-consuming paths, including timer AND direct dispatch, hold shared authority from standing read through commitment. Stopping only a timer or returning a nonzero exit cannot prevent another actuator from consuming partial state. BeltRunState.Paused was only a constant planner input, not a host inhibition mechanism."),
    p(text: "**Identity:** the release is binary B executing tree T under contract C, not any one revision/hash. Build digest plus cutover reread now supplies executable identity; service_contract_identity still lacks an observer and remains unauthored. The prior deployed_release_binding observable pair was only a transition guard: --release-revision is a launch argument, so restarting an old binary with a new unit can announce the new revision. Its added expected surface check/ReleaseSurfaceMixed refuses changed rendering, but identical rendering is served-release agreement, not executable provenance."),
    p(text: "**Refuted repairs:** DeploymentArtifactStep contained kind/path, keyed by path; kind was fixed by the spec, so structural value_eq could never produce MemberChanged. Adding an observed provider would yield empty plans over stale content, regressing unconditional apply. CandidateRelease owns WHAT while DeploymentSpec owns WHERE; candidate-sensitive members require modeled replacement for every kind before EffectReplace can stop poisoning. Revision-only catch-up is also unsafe: updating the tree opens belt_revision_admission while the old binary runs. The prior local revision remains a CAS baseline, not deployment truth. Readiness must run even on a no-op plan, and transaction locking must cover all consumers, not merely concurrent deploy runs."),
    p(text: "**Standing ownership rule:** retract is spec-driven and NEVER takes host observation as ownership. Present bytes cannot establish who wrote them; process release identity and stored install manifests do not supply the refused provenance authority. Apply observations are per desired member and cannot construct observed-only keys, making removal unreachable structurally. A separate unowned-state census may enumerate beyond the roster but must never feed the diff. It is shelved, not disproven: classify runtime-created state by class, not instance; a singleton is provisional until a second instance or typed disposition identifies the class. Track provisional→class, widening and disappearance. Independently prove discovery sees worktree-registration grain before any monotone baseline; a host snapshot alone is not an oracle."),
    p(text: "**Observation subject:** admitted ReleaseRevisionBinding originally reached ServeBinary’s announcement but GunbcSourceTree copied ambient checkout bytes. Readiness comparing two copies of the admitted value cannot validate publication. Wrong-commit tracked paths evade candidate path admission, while revision readback exposes perpetual drift. path_is_deployed/deployed_tree_excluded_prefixes is the shared scope authority; raw-source versus deployed-projection hashes are incomparable. Any projection identity must derive exclusions from that authority, never accept caller-supplied scope; changing scope is a typed mass rebaseline. .git is outside byte identity: prove HEAD names the admitted revision and that revision is reachable, not object-store equality."),
    p(text: "**Transport/subject join:** lift git argv out of RevParseInRepo’s local transport literal; consume the same words locally and through shape_fleet_ssh_exec/ssh.Session.ExecPortableWords. No Remote operation nickname or new SSH transport kind. Both revision and content observations use ONE FleetSshExecutionContext with credential, attempt-owned known-host anchor and portable alphabet; joined observations carry its sealed locus so cross-host combinations are unconstructible. Local and remote observations remain one shape/two realizations."),
    p(text: "**Instrument history and current scope:** a shell-pipeline digest violates portable words; ordinary status/diff-index/diff-files misread the deliberately unsynced .git/index. git diff HEAD discriminated tracked bytes against scratch repos with stale/correct indexes, corrupt content and same-size mtime-restored changes; ctime prevents the rsync-preserved-mtime false clean in that measured route. But tracked-only checks missed target untracked/ignored deployed source: source candidate admission does not own the target. The repair uses status --porcelain=v1 -z --untracked-files=all and ls-files --others --ignored --exclude-standard -z under the SAME temporary index, calling scan_checkout_status and scan_ignored_deployed_paths with path_is_deployed. Malformed fields or an owed rename source give ContentUnreadable→TreeStatusUnreadable, never empty clean."),
    p(text: "**Observation limits:** gitattributes normalization could conceal byte differences; gunbc.gitattributes_emit must refuse clean/smudge, text=auto, eol and working-tree-encoding rather than rely on their measured absence. Classify command failures such as missing repository versus unreadable .git instead of flattening them to String causes. Sealed locus gives one host, not atomic time: the four reads can race. Temporary-index binding narrows races toward divergence, but only the deployment execution lease closes atomic observation. Do not invent a second porcelain grammar as a substitute."),
    p(text: "**Validate:** fixed key + changed meaningful state must produce MemberChanged; equal states must also be reachable. Target untracked/ignored deployed files diverge, while target/release/gunbc and target/debug/x remain converged because excluded scope is independently authoritative. Test drift precedence, distinct standing arms, malformed reads, wrong-host unconstructibility, portable-pipe refusal, mixed surface, admission across both belt ingresses, failed cutover recovery, no-op readiness and rollback to N-1. Pure-fold witnesses and scratch git commands do not prove the actual SSH/report/receipt path: deployed_tree_report_ci_wet was unexecuted in the audit and needs a real srv1 receipt/authorized workflow consumer. [Original refutations and evidence](https://github.com/gunb-ai/gunbc/blob/06d3c1c3149a7420c4a548b9aba9b15c721c635d/dag/gunbc/plans/deploy_convergence_observed_side.dag) remain historical. The recurring degenerate-input class needs its own guarantee-census control; no green witness total closes that obligation."),
  ]
}

data deploy_convergence_observed_side_plan: Plan = Plan {
  projection: PlanIsAuthorityOnly { ruling: project_documentation_projections_are_not_checkout_artifacts },
  slug: "deploy-convergence-observed-side",
  title: "Deploy convergence: the observed side, and why the obvious repair is a regression",
  body: deploy_convergence_observed_side_body(),
  retirement: PlanRetiresWhen {
    condition: unbound_dissolution(
      description: "Delete this record when the tree-publication vertical is live and its lessons have moved to the carriers that enforce them: the deployed-tree observation green-by-execution against srv1 with a receipt; publication constructible only through a quiescence guard that proves the belt TIMER and SERVICE inactive with no queued or running invocation; belt admission consuming a release identity that binds binary to tree rather than repository revision alone; every live-deploy member launching from an immutable release generation with an active-generation record, staged on the srv1 lab before cutover, with an executed rollback-to-N-1 witness; and the recurring class -- a predicate true by construction under a degenerate input, still asserted by its type when the input becomes real -- carried as a row in the guarantee census with its own discriminating control. Until every one of those holds, this is the only place the refuted branch, the instrument history and the belt-enablement finding are written down, and deleting it would cost the next author the same three nights.",
    ),
  },
}
